WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.530 --> 00:00:03.580
- [Narrator] The flu
is a highly contagious

00:00:03.580 --> 00:00:05.223
respiratory illness.

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:10.343
It turns up year after year
with devastating consequences.

00:00:11.230 --> 00:00:14.423
All caused by a most elusive virus.

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:21.770
The influenza, or flu virus,
is a recurring nightmare.

00:00:21.770 --> 00:00:25.920
It causes more than 36,000
deaths in the U.S. each year.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:27.450
And was responsible for some

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:30.309
of history's deadliest pandemics.

00:00:30.309 --> 00:00:34.810
Like other viruses, the
flu virus is a parasite.

00:00:34.810 --> 00:00:38.250
The viral agent itself called, a virion,

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:43.250
is made of ribonucleic acid
or RNA surrounded by proteins.

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:48.810
The flu virus uses two
proteins to attack its host,

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:53.810
hemagglutinin and neuraminidase
or the HA and NA proteins.

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:59.940
The HA protein attaches a
virus to a cell and lets it in.

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:03.339
Once the virus is inside, it multiplies.

00:01:03.339 --> 00:01:07.510
Then the NA protein cuts
this viral swarm loose,

00:01:07.510 --> 00:01:10.143
sending it off to infect more cells.

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:14.500
This triggers the immune
system to bombard the swarm

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:16.360
and to destroy infected tissue

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:18.313
throughout the respiratory system.

00:01:19.510 --> 00:01:22.920
Unfortunately, this response
can also inadvertently

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:25.220
lead to death through organ failure

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:28.453
or secondary infections
like bacterial pneumonia.

00:01:32.630 --> 00:01:36.580
The flu virus has been nearly
impossible to eradicate.

00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:40.320
Largely due to its
uncanny ability to mutate.

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:44.620
Since just 2004, more than
5,000 different strains

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:47.223
of the influenza virus
have been sequenced.

00:01:48.230 --> 00:01:50.380
Those that affect humans are categorized

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:53.140
as Types A, B or C.

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:55.860
With Type A strains being the most capable

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:58.150
of unleashing a pandemic.

00:01:58.150 --> 00:02:01.730
Influenza A viruses mutate more rapidly

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:06.140
allowing them to adapt to new
hosts and even cross species.

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:08.640
Avian flu and Swine flu, for instance,

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:11.270
are two strains of Type A viruses

00:02:11.270 --> 00:02:14.270
that through mutations, can be transmitted

00:02:14.270 --> 00:02:17.273
from birds and pigs to humans.

00:02:20.990 --> 00:02:23.030
One of the deadliest flu pandemics

00:02:23.030 --> 00:02:25.303
was caused one such virus.

00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:29.320
Between 1918 and 1919,

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:33.100
the Spanish flu infected a
third of the global population

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:37.410
and killed up to 50
million people worldwide.

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:41.140
Called H1N1, this
particular strain of virus

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:43.293
likely came from birds.

00:02:46.730 --> 00:02:50.053
The threat of another
influenza pandemic remains.

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:54.270
In the meantime, scientists
are constantly monitoring

00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:57.790
the flu virus and
developing seasonal vaccines

00:02:57.790 --> 00:03:01.423
to create our best line of defense.

